  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How does an ion engine push a spacecraft?",
      "a": "It turns xenon gas into charged ions and uses electrically charged grids to fire them out of the back extremely fast. By Newton's third law, throwing the ions backwards pushes the spacecraft forwards.",
      "asks": [
        "How do ion thrusters work?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is an ion?",
      "a": "An ion is an atom that has gained or lost electrons, so it carries an electric charge. Xenon atoms that lose an electron become positive ions that electric fields can push.",
      "asks": [
        "What does ionised mean?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Where does an ion engine get its power?",
      "a": "Usually from large solar panels. That is why ion engines suit missions in the inner solar system, where sunlight is strong; far from the Sun there is much less solar power.",
      "asks": [
        "Do ion engines need electricity?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does an ion engine need a neutraliser?",
      "a": "Firing out only positive ions would leave the spacecraft negatively charged, pulling the ions back. A neutraliser sprays electrons into the beam to keep everything balanced.",
      "asks": [
        "What does the neutraliser do?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why not use ion engines to launch from Earth?",
      "a": "Their push is far too weak to lift anything against Earth's gravity; the weight of a sheet of paper can't lift a rocket. They only work in space, where even a tiny push adds up over months.",
      "asks": [
        "Can an ion engine launch a rocket from Earth?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "NASA's Dawn spacecraft used ion engines to orbit both the asteroid Vesta and the dwarf planet Ceres.",
    "Ion engines can be roughly ten times more fuel-efficient than chemical rockets."
  ],
